"# Wikipedia Article Inventory: The Mandela Effect and Its Two Candidate Explanations\n\nSearches of English Wikipedia returned **no standalone article titled \"Mandela effect.\"** The page `Mandela effect` is a **redirect to \"False memory\"**, which hosts a dedicated \"Mandela effect\" section as the canonical treatment of the phenomenon [1][5]. Below is the full inventory of retrieved pages, organized by role.\n\n---\n\n## A. The Phenomenon Itself\n\n| # | Page Title | URL | Notes |\n|---|-----------|-----|-------|\n| 1 | **False memory** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_memory | Canonical landing page for \"Mandela effect\" (redirect target). Contains a dedicated \"Mandela effect\" section: a phenomenon in which a large group of people share the same false memory, coined by Fiona Broome (c. 2009) after she and others distinctly remembered Nelson Mandela dying in prison in the 1980s — he in fact died in 2013. Cites other examples: \"Berenstain/Berenstein Bears,\" the Monopoly monocle, misquoted movie lines (\"Luke, I am your father\"), the phantom \"Shazaam\" film [1][5] |\n| 2 | **Berenstain Bears** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berenstain_Bears | Documents the persistent \"Berenstein\" misspelling and explicitly frames it as a commonly cited example of the Mandela effect / collective false memory [2] |\n| 3 | **Nelson Mandela** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nelson_Mandela | Biography; supplies the factual anchor — death on **5 December 2013** (South African president 1994–1999) — that contradicts the shared false memory giving the phenomenon its name. Does not itself discuss the Mandela effect [3] |\n\n---\n\n## B. Candidate Explanation 1 — Memory-Based (Psychological)\n\nThe six main articles documenting how groups of people form *identical* false memories:\n\n| # | Page Title | URL | How it explains shared false memory |\n|---|-----------|-----|-------------------------------------|\n| 4 | **False memory** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_memory | Memories are reconstructive, not exact recordings; circulating misinformation, suggestion, and imagination can be encoded by many people as the same vivid but inaccurate recollection [1] |\n| 5 | **Confabulation** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confabulation | The brain unconsciously fills memory gaps with plausible fabricated content; people exposed to the same gaps and cultural cues independently produce the same confident false account |\n| 6 | **Misinformation effect** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Misinformation_effect | Drawing on Elizabeth Loftus's research: post-event misleading information is integrated into the original memory, so a group encountering the same erroneous claims incorporates identical errors |\n| 7 | **Source-monitoring error** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-monitoring_error | Based on Marcia Johnson's source-monitoring framework: people misattribute where a memory came from, mistaking things heard/read/imagined for things experienced — the same misattribution across many individuals |\n| 8 | **Cryptomnesia** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptomnesia | A forgotten memory resurfaces as a seemingly genuine \"original\" impression without awareness of its true source; a real-but-misremembered detail is sincerely recalled by many as fact |\n| 9 | **Memory conformity** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_conformity | Also called social contagion of memory: after discussing an event, people align recollections with one another, so one person's error spreads through the group until members share the same false memory |\n\n---\n\n## C. Candidate Explanation 2 — Multiverse / Parallel Worlds\n\nThe articles covering the parallel-realities idea popularly invoked to \"explain\" the Mandela effect. **Notably, none of them mentions the Mandela effect** — the multiverse pages treat it as a physics/cosmology or fiction topic, and the Mandela-effect content lives only on \"False memory\":\n\n| # | Page Title | URL | Coverage | Mandela effect mentioned? |\n|---|-----------|-----|----------|---------------------------|\n| 10 | **Multiverse** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiverse | Hypothetical ensemble of multiple universes: Tegmark's Level I–IV taxonomy, eternal inflation/bubble universes, string-theory landscape, braneworlds; debate over testability | No (delegates fiction to \"Parallel universes in fiction\" via hatnote) |\n| 11 | **Many-worlds interpretation** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Many-worlds_interpretation | Hugh Everett III's relative-state formulation of QM — all quantum outcomes realized in branching worlds; debates over probability, decoherence, objections | No |\n| 12 | **Parallel universes in fiction** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallel_universes_in_fiction | Parallel/alternate worlds as a storytelling device from antiquity through modern SF, comics (DC's multiverse), film, TV | No |\n| 13 | **Parallel universe (disambiguation)** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallel_universe_(disambiguation) | Disambiguation page sorting uses of \"parallel universe\"; points to #10–#12 | No |\n| 14 | **Alternate history** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternate_history | Counterfactual-history genre: divergence points, alternate timelines in fiction | No |\n\n---\n\n## D. Contextual Articles (Origin and Framing of the Term)\n\n| # | Page Title | URL | Relevance |\n|---|-----------|-----|-----------|\n| 15 | **Collective memory** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collective_memory | Scholarly framing for how groups share, maintain, and distort memories over time — the primary academic lens for mass misremembering |\n| 16 | **Social contagion** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_contagion | How beliefs, emotions, and false claims spread through populations via social influence — the transmission mechanism by which a mistaken memory becomes widely adopted |\n| 17 | **List of common misconceptions** | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_common_misconceptions | The main list-style article cataloguing widely held but false beliefs; closest on-wiki analogue for documenting popular misrememberings |\n\n---\n\n## Key Structural Finding\n\nWikipedia's editorial posture is itself informative for the two-explanation question:\n\n1. **The phenomenon is filed under psychology, not physics.** \"Mandela effect\" redirects to **False memory** [1], and the multiverse articles (#10–#12) contain no Mandela-effect content. Wikipedia thus implicitly endorses the memory-based explanation as the encyclopedic framing, treating the multiverse idea as a popular (unendorsed) interpretation.\n2. **The memory-based explanation has a deep, well-developed article cluster** (#4–#9, #15–#16) with named researchers (Loftus, Johnson) and established mechanisms (confabulation, misinformation, source monitoring, conformity).\n3. **The multiverse explanation is represented only by general science/fiction articles** (#10–#14) that never connect to the phenomenon.\n\n**Caveat:** This session could not live-fetch full pages; findings reflect the last stable recorded versions of these pages (through early 2025). The redirect of \"Mandela effect\" → \"False memory\" and the article set above have been stable for years, but a marginal \"see also\" mention elsewhere could theoretically have shifted."